A||||5||sloth85GT||Getting steering wheel off???||||5||
Z||000000||sloth85GT||12-30-2001||02:00 PM||||I did some searching and found that in order to remove the steering wheel you need to take that huge nut off, then there's supposed to be a C-clip somewhere, I didn't see one at all. I have an 85 GT with the 3-poke wheel. Am I missing something? How do I get this damn thing off? I haven't tried a wheel puller yet, I know I'll need one, that's why I'm headed out to Canadian Tire right after this to take advantage of their loan-a-tool program. Can someone please help me out here, I'd like to get the wheel off today, but if not, it can wait, my car isn't going anywhere. <P>And on an unrelated side note, my car which has been sitting in my driveway in the freezing Winnipeg cold for about a month without being started, fired right up at the turn of the key. WOW! And did I mention that my motor also has a spun rod bearing? And no I'm not driving it, waiting to do the LT1 swap.<P>Thanks to anyone and everyone who replies  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.mts.net/~kuzma/fieronl.gif">      <BR>Dan<BR>1985 Silver GT<P>LT1 Coming Soon.....||||reg||5
Z||000001||Adrift||12-30-2001||02:04 PM||||The spark plug socket from my rachet set took off that huge bolt, it was the perfect size... after that just use the steering wheel puller and thats it! ||||reg||1
Z||000002||DXR_DAD||12-30-2001||03:12 PM||||There should be a little "C" type clip that needs to be removed before you can take off the nut?? It may be missing......It's just there to keep the nut on if it loosens up<P>And yes a wheel puller will make it easy!!!<BR>Good luck!! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>DXR_DAD||||reg||4
Z||000003||topcat||12-30-2001||03:43 PM||||If you get one of those free loaner tools, inspect the threads on the bolts before leaving the store.  I got one about a month ago, and when I started threading the bolts, one did not want to go in.  I checked the threads, and the bolt threads were a little worn.  I could not get the bolt in more than three turns.  When I started pulling, the bolt broke, need I say more.... the bolt is stuck, and it is going to take some drilling to get it out.  I have not messed with it since.  Too busy at work, but I need to swap the steering wheel after it comes out of th epaint shop.  The next time I use a puller it will have steel bolts, not the aluminum ones that came wit the loaner.  just my .02 cents||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||12-30-2001||03:52 PM||||There is info in my cave on this plus a link to Oliver's site which has good pictures.<P>There are usually 2 clips installed by the factory....<P>Clip one, if it's still there, is on the tip of the steering shaft. It has to come off before the nut will...<P>Any deep socket set can reach the nut. I don't remember if it's 11/16 or 3/4....<P>Steering wheel puller is next... As indicated above, make sure the loaner ones haven't been trashed before leaving the store. Same for loaner lock plat tools.<P>The next clip is on the lock plate. you need a lock plate tool to depress the plate and get off that clip.<P>Note, check the threads on the steering shaft before using the lock plate tool... They often aren't cut that well. Don't force the lock plate tool's sleeve over crappy threads.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000005||sloth85GT||12-30-2001||04:19 PM||||I got the nut off, that was easy, it was just that from my previous understanding, the c-clip was to come off AFTER the bolt does. Now if it was supposed to come off before the bolt, mine must have been missing, I didn't see one. I got the steering wheel puller and I'm gonna go try that in a few minutes, so hopefully all will go well, I don't anticipate any further problems. ||||reg||1
